<!-- and a long <description> for screen readers.                          -->

<section xml:id="sec-vector-fields">
  <title>Vector Fields and Line Integrals</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this section we meet two ideas. A <em>vector field</em> attaches a
      vector<mdash/>a magnitude and a direction<mdash/>to every point of the
      plane or of space; think of the velocity of the air at each point of a
      room, or the gravitational pull at each point around a planet. The
      <em>line integral of a vector field</em> then answers a natural
      question: as an object moves along a curve through the field, how much
      does the field push it along its way? The physical name for this
      accumulated push is <em>work</em>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-vector-fields">
    <title>Vector Fields</title>

    <p>
      What is a vector field? We will study the definition through an
      example. Assume that we have a function <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y)</m> on
      <m>\R^2</m>: the function <m>\mathbf{F}</m> takes two real numbers
      <m>(x,y)</m> as its input and outputs a vector in <m>\R^2</m>. As an
      example, consider the following vector field:
      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-radial-field" number="yes">
          \mathbf{F}(x,y)=\left\langle x,\ y\right\rangle.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      To visualize this vector field, imagine that at each point
      <m>(x,y)</m> there is a vector <m>\left\langle x,\ y\right\rangle</m>
      attached. Physically, this vector could correspond to the velocity of
      an atom located at the point <m>(x,y)</m>. See
      <xref ref="fig-radial-field"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-radial-field">
      <caption>
        The vector field <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\left\langle x,\ y\right\rangle</m>.
        Every arrow points directly away from the origin, and the arrows get
        longer as the distance from the origin grows.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-radial-field" width="60%">
        <shortdescription>
          A grid of arrows all pointing away from the origin, growing longer
          farther from the origin.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            The plane with x and y from minus 5 to 5. At each point of a
            regular grid a blue arrow points radially outward, directly away
            from the origin. Arrows near the origin are short; arrows near
            the edge of the picture are long. A red dot marks the origin,
            where the field is the zero vector.
          </p>
        </description>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="radial-field">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
            <definition>F(x,y) = (0.22*x, 0.22*y)</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-5.4,-5.4,5.4,5.4)" aspect-ratio="1">
              <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>

              <group at="field">
                <repeat parameter="i=-4..4">
                  <repeat parameter="j=-4..4">
                    <vector v="F(i,j)" tail="(i,j)" stroke="blue"
                            thickness="2"/>
                  </repeat>
                </repeat>
              </group>

              <group at="origin">
                <point p="(0,0)" size="4.5" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
              </group>
            </coordinates>

            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure"
                          text="The vector field F of x and y equals the
                                vector x, y: at every point of a grid an
                                arrow points straight away from the origin,
                                growing longer with the distance from it">
                <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                <annotation ref="field"
                            text="The grid of field vectors, each pointing
                                  radially outward with length equal to the
                                  distance from the origin"/>
                <annotation ref="origin"
                            text="The origin, where the field is the zero
                                  vector"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Note that the length of the vector in
      <xref ref="eq-radial-field"/> is <m>\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>, the distance
      from the origin. This is why
      the blue arrows in <xref ref="fig-radial-field"/> grow in length as we
      move away from the origin along a straight line. Can we modify the
      vector field so that the length of the vectors is one at every point?
      The answer is yes: dividing the vector by its own length produces a
      unit vector, and hence the vector field
      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-unit-radial-field" number="yes">
          \mathbf{F}(x,y)
            =\frac{\left\langle x,\ y\right\rangle}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}},
        </mrow>
      </md>
      of <xref ref="eq-unit-radial-field"/>, when visualized, looks like
      <xref ref="fig-unit-radial-field"/>: the
      directions are unchanged, but now all arrows have the same length.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-unit-radial-field">
      <caption>
        The normalized field
        <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\dfrac{\left\langle x,\ y\right\rangle}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}</m>:
        same directions as <xref ref="fig-radial-field"/>, but every arrow
        has length one.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-unit-radial-field" width="60%">
        <shortdescription>
          A grid of arrows all pointing away from the origin, all of the
          same length.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            The plane with x and y from minus 5 to 5. At each point of a
            regular grid a blue arrow points radially outward, directly away
            from the origin, and every arrow has exactly the same length. A
            red dot marks the origin, where the field is undefined.
          </p>
        </description>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="unit-radial-field">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
            <!-- the tiny constant keeps the origin, where the field is
                 undefined, from dividing by zero -->
            <definition>U(x,y) = (0.55*x/sqrt(x^2+y^2+0.0001),
                                  0.55*y/sqrt(x^2+y^2+0.0001))</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-5.4,-5.4,5.4,5.4)" aspect-ratio="1">
              <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>

              <group at="field">
                <repeat parameter="i=-4..4">
                  <repeat parameter="j=-4..4">
                    <vector v="U(i,j)" tail="(i,j)" stroke="blue"
                            thickness="2"/>
                  </repeat>
                </repeat>
              </group>

              <group at="origin">
                <point p="(0,0)" size="4.5" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
              </group>
            </coordinates>

            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure"
                          text="The normalized field: at every point of a
                                grid an arrow points straight away from the
                                origin, and now every arrow has the same
                                length">
                <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                <annotation ref="field"
                            text="The grid of unit field vectors, all of the
                                  same length, each pointing radially
                                  outward"/>
                <annotation ref="origin"
                            text="The origin, where the field is undefined"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Our next example is a vector field which is a function on
      <m>\R^3</m>. Consider the following vector field:
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \mathbf{F}(x,y,z) \amp
            = \frac{\left\langle -x,\ -y,\ -z\right\rangle}
                   {\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{3/2}}
        </mrow>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-gravity-field" number="yes">
          \amp = -\frac{\mathbf{r}}{\left\lVert\mathbf{r}\right\rVert^3},
                 \qquad
                 \mathbf{r}=\left\langle x,\ y,\ z\right\rangle.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      This can, for instance, represent the gravitational field of a massive
      body located at the origin: every arrow points <em>toward</em> the
      origin, and since
      <m>\left\lVert\mathbf{F}\right\rVert
         =\left\lVert\mathbf{r}\right\rVert/\left\lVert\mathbf{r}\right\rVert^3
         =1/\left\lVert\mathbf{r}\right\rVert^2</m>,
      the pull weakens with the square of the distance<mdash/>the
      inverse-square law. See <xref ref="fig-gravity-field"/>. We will meet
      this field again in <xref ref="ex-helix-gravity"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-gravity-field">
      <caption>
        The gravitational field
        <m>\mathbf{F}=-\mathbf{r}/\left\lVert\mathbf{r}\right\rVert^3</m>. All arrows
        point toward the origin and shrink with the square of the distance.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-gravity-field" width="70%">
        <shortdescription>
          A three-dimensional grid of arrows all pointing toward the origin,
          longer near the origin.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A three-dimensional box with x, y, and z from minus 0.7 to 0.7.
            At each point of a coarse three-dimensional grid a blue arrow
            points directly toward the center of the box, where a red dot
            marks the origin. Arrows close to the origin are long; arrows at
            the corners of the box are short.
          </p>
        </description>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[width=10cm, view={120}{22},
              xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
              xmin=-0.7, xmax=0.7, ymin=-0.7, ymax=0.7, zmin=-0.7, zmax=0.7,
              grid=major, grid style={gray!20}]
            \addplot3[blue, line width=1.2pt, -{Stealth[length=5pt]},
                quiver={u=\thisrow{u}, v=\thisrow{v}, w=\thisrow{w}}]
                table[row sep=\\] {%
x y z u v w\\
-0.5 -0.5 -0.5 0.035 0.035 0.035\\
-0.5 -0.5 0 0.064 0.064 -0.000\\
-0.5 -0.5 0.5 0.035 0.035 -0.035\\
-0.5 0 -0.5 0.064 -0.000 0.064\\
-0.5 0 0 0.180 -0.000 -0.000\\
-0.5 0 0.5 0.064 -0.000 -0.064\\
-0.5 0.5 -0.5 0.035 -0.035 0.035\\
-0.5 0.5 0 0.064 -0.064 -0.000\\
-0.5 0.5 0.5 0.035 -0.035 -0.035\\
0 -0.5 -0.5 -0.000 0.064 0.064\\
0 -0.5 0 -0.000 0.180 -0.000\\
0 -0.5 0.5 -0.000 0.064 -0.064\\
0 0 -0.5 -0.000 -0.000 0.180\\
0 0 0.5 -0.000 -0.000 -0.180\\
0 0.5 -0.5 -0.000 -0.064 0.064\\
0 0.5 0 -0.000 -0.180 -0.000\\
0 0.5 0.5 -0.000 -0.064 -0.064\\
0.5 -0.5 -0.5 -0.035 0.035 0.035\\
0.5 -0.5 0 -0.064 0.064 -0.000\\
0.5 -0.5 0.5 -0.035 0.035 -0.035\\
0.5 0 -0.5 -0.064 -0.000 0.064\\
0.5 0 0 -0.180 -0.000 -0.000\\
0.5 0 0.5 -0.064 -0.000 -0.064\\
0.5 0.5 -0.5 -0.035 -0.035 0.035\\
0.5 0.5 0 -0.064 -0.064 -0.000\\
0.5 0.5 0.5 -0.035 -0.035 -0.035\\
};
            \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, red, mark size=1.5pt] coordinates {(0,0,0)};
          \end{axis}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-work-reminder">
    <title>Work (Reminder)</title>

    <p>
      Our goal is to study the line integral of a vector field over a curve
      <m>C</m>, and we will do so using an example from physics: calculating
      the amount of work done by a force <m>\mathbf{F}</m>, which in general
      can be a function of <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m>. Before we
      present the example, let us remind ourselves of the definition of
      work. Consider a box being pulled by a constant force
      <m>\mathbf{F}</m>, as shown in <xref ref="fig-work-reminder"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-work-reminder">
      <caption>
        A box pulled by a constant force <m>\mathbf{F}</m> applied at an
        angle <m>\alpha</m>. The displacement <m>\delta x</m> is measured
        below the ground line, from the centre of mass of the box in its
        initial position to its centre of mass in the final position (the
        faint box). Only the component
        <m>\left\lVert\mathbf{F}\right\rVert\cos(\alpha)\,\mathbf{i}</m>
        along the motion does work.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-work-reminder" width="80%">
        <shortdescription>
          A box on a horizontal line pulled by a tilted force arrow, with
          the horizontal component of the force and the displacement marked.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A horizontal line represents the ground, with a small box
            sitting on it at the left. A red arrow labelled F points up and
            to the right from the top of the box, making an angle alpha with
            the horizontal; a small arc marks the angle. A shorter blue
            horizontal arrow labelled the magnitude of F times cosine alpha,
            times i, shows the horizontal component of the force. A faint
            second box to the right marks the final position. The centre of
            each box is marked with a dot, and a dashed vertical line drops
            from each centre to a dashed double-headed arrow drawn below the
            ground line and labelled delta x, so the displacement is
            measured from one centre of mass to the other.
          </p>
        </description>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="work-reminder">
          <diagram dimensions="(560,300)" margins="5">
            <coordinates bbox="(-0.8,-1.7,6.7,3.0)" aspect-ratio="1">
              <group at="ground">
                <line endpoints="((-0.5,0), (6.3,0))" stroke="black"
                      thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
              </group>

              <group at="boxes">
                <rectangle lower-left="(0.3,0)" dimensions="(0.5,0.6)"
                           stroke="black" fill="#d9d9d9" thickness="2"/>
                <rectangle lower-left="(4.9,0)" dimensions="(0.5,0.6)"
                           stroke="#b3b3b3" fill="#f2f2f2" thickness="2"/>
              </group>

              <group at="force">
                <vector v="(1.157,1.379)" tail="(0.55,0.6)" stroke="red"
                        thickness="3" arrow-width="6"/>
                <label anchor="(1.78,2.02)" alignment="e" color="red">
                  <m>\mathbf{F}</m>
                </label>
                <arc center="(0.55,0.6)" radius="0.62" range="(0,50)"
                     stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
                <label anchor="(1.58,1.02)" alignment="c">
                  <m>\alpha</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <group at="component">
                <vector v="(1.157,0)" tail="(0.55,0.6)" stroke="blue"
                        thickness="3" arrow-width="6"/>
                <label anchor="(1.80,0.30)" alignment="e" color="blue">
                  <m>\left\lVert\mathbf{F}\right\rVert\cos(\alpha)\,\mathbf{i}</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <group at="displacement">
                <point p="(0.55,0.3)" size="3.5" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                <point p="(5.15,0.3)" size="3.5" stroke="#8c8c8c"
                       fill="#8c8c8c"/>
                <line endpoints="((0.55,0.3), (0.55,-0.85))" stroke="#8c8c8c"
                      thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
                <line endpoints="((5.15,0.3), (5.15,-0.85))" stroke="#8c8c8c"
                      thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
                <line endpoints="((0.55,-0.68), (5.15,-0.68))"
                      stroke="#009E73" thickness="3" dash="7 4" arrows="2"/>
                <label anchor="(2.85,-1.2)" alignment="c" color="#009E73">
                  <m>\delta x</m>
                </label>
              </group>
            </coordinates>

            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure"
                          text="A box on the ground pulled by a force applied
                                at an angle alpha, with the displacement
                                measured from one centre of mass to the
                                other">
                <annotation ref="ground" text="The ground the box slides on"/>
                <annotation ref="boxes"
                            text="The box in its initial position and, faintly,
                                  in its final position"/>
                <annotation ref="force"
                            text="The force F, applied at an angle alpha to
                                  the direction of motion"/>
                <annotation ref="component"
                            text="The component of the force along the
                                  motion, the magnitude of F times cosine
                                  alpha"/>
                <annotation ref="displacement"
                            text="The displacement delta x, measured below the
                                  ground line from the centre of mass of the
                                  box in its initial position to its centre of
                                  mass in the final position"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The amount of work done by the force is equal to the magnitude of the
      force in the direction of motion,
      <m>\left\lVert\mathbf{F}\right\rVert\cos(\alpha)</m>, multiplied by the amount
      of displacement <m>\delta x</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-work-constant" number="yes">
          \text{Work}
            =\left\lVert\mathbf{F}\right\rVert\cos(\alpha)\times\delta x
            =\mathbf{F}\cdot\left(\delta x\,\mathbf{i}\right).
        </mrow>
      </md>
      The last equality holds because the motion is in the
      <m>+x</m>-direction, which lets us rewrite
      <m>\left\lVert\mathbf{F}\right\rVert\cos(\alpha)\times\delta x</m> as the dot
      product <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot(\delta x\,\mathbf{i})</m>. This dot-product
      form is the one that generalizes: <em>work is the dot product of the
      force with the displacement</em>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-work-force-field">
    <title>Work of a Force Field</title>

    <p>
      Let us now consider the situation where a force <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y)</m>
      is acting on an object and causing it to move along a curved
      trajectory from an initial point to a final point. Neither the force
      nor the direction of motion is constant anymore, so we cannot apply
      <xref ref="eq-work-constant"/> directly. Instead, we reuse the strategy of
      <xref ref="sec-line-integrals"/>: calculate the work on small subarcs
      of the trajectory, where force and direction are nearly constant, and
      then sum up. <xref ref="fig-work-subarc"/> shows one such subarc.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-work-subarc">
      <caption>
        On the <m>k</m>th subarc, of length <m>\Delta s_k</m>, the direction
        of motion is the unit tangent vector <m>\mathbf{T}_k</m> (green) and
        the force is approximately <m>\mathbf{F}(x_k,y_k)</m> (red). Only
        the tangential component of the force does work.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-work-subarc" width="55%">
        <shortdescription>
          A curved trajectory with one short subarc highlighted, showing the
          force vector and the unit tangent vector at a point.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A blue curve rises from a red point near the origin, bending
            upward to a red endpoint at the upper right. A short piece of
            the curve, labelled Delta s sub k, is drawn thicker in dark red.
            At a black point on this piece, a red arrow labelled F of (x sub
            k, y sub k) points steeply up and to the right, and a shorter
            green arrow labelled T sub k points along the curve in the
            direction of motion.
          </p>
        </description>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="work-subarc">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,420)" margins="5">
            <definition>q(t) = (t, 0.5+0.5*t^2)</definition>
            <definition>tk = 0.52</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-0.35,-0.35,3.1,3.2)" aspect-ratio="1">
              <axes at="ax" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"
                    arrows="1"/>

              <group at="curve">
                <parametric-curve function="q" domain="(0,2)" stroke="blue"
                                  thickness="3"/>
              </group>

              <group at="ends">
                <point p="q(0)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                <point p="q(2)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
              </group>

              <group at="subarc">
                <parametric-curve function="q" domain="(0.34,0.74)"
                                  stroke="deeppink" thickness="8"/>
                <label anchor="(0.55,0.30)" alignment="center" color="deeppink">
                  <m>\Delta s_k</m>
                </label>
                <line endpoints="((0.55,0.40), (0.55,0.58))"
                      stroke="deeppink" thickness="1"/>
              </group>

              <group at="vectors">
                <point p="q(tk)" size="4" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                <vector v="(0.42,1.19)" tail="q(tk)" stroke="red"
                        thickness="3" arrow-width="6"/>
                <label anchor="(0.94,1.86)" alignment="nw" color="red">
                  <m>\mathbf{F}(x_k,y_k)</m>
                </label>
                <vector v="(0.78,0.41)" tail="q(tk)" stroke="#009E73"
                        thickness="3" arrow-width="6"/>
                <label anchor="(1.32,1.03)" alignment="e" offset="(6,-4)"
                       color="#009E73">
                  <m>\mathbf{T}_k</m>
                </label>
              </group>
            </coordinates>

            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure"
                          text="A curved trajectory with one short subarc
                                highlighted, carrying the force vector and the
                                unit tangent vector at a sample point">
                <annotation ref="ax" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                <annotation ref="curve"
                            text="The trajectory of the object"
                            sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="ends"
                            text="The initial and final points of the
                                  trajectory"/>
                <annotation ref="subarc"
                            text="The kth subarc, of length delta s sub k,
                                  drawn in pink"/>
                <annotation ref="vectors"
                            text="At a sample point of that subarc, the force
                                  F of x sub k, y sub k and the unit tangent
                                  vector T sub k, which gives the direction of
                                  motion"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      On the <m>k</m>th subarc the displacement has length
      <m>\Delta s_k</m> and direction <m>\mathbf{T}_k</m>, the unit tangent
      vector, so by the dot-product formula for work, the work done along
      the <m>k</m>th subarc is
      <m>\mathbf{F}(x_k,y_k)\cdot\mathbf{T}_k\,\Delta s_k</m>. Therefore the
      work done over the whole curve can be approximated as
      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-work-sum" number="yes">
          W\approx\sum_{k=1}^{n}
            \mathbf{F}(x_k,y_k)\cdot\mathbf{T}_k\,\Delta s_k.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Finally, to find the exact amount of work we take the limit of
      <xref ref="eq-work-sum"/> as the number of subarcs approaches
      infinity:
      <md>
        <mrow>
          W \amp =\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^{n}
                  \mathbf{F}(x_k,y_k)\cdot\mathbf{T}_k\,\Delta s_k
        </mrow>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-work-Tds" number="yes">
          \amp =\int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{T}\,ds,
        </mrow>
      </md>
      where <m>\mathbf{T}</m> is the unit tangent vector of the curve. Since
      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-unit-tangent" number="yes">
          \mathbf{T}=\frac{\mathbf{v}}{\left\lVert\mathbf{v}\right\rVert}
            =\frac{\frac{d\mathbf{r}}{dt}}{\frac{ds}{dt}}
            =\frac{d\mathbf{r}}{ds},
        </mrow>
      </md>
      the combination <m>\mathbf{T}\,ds</m> in
      <xref ref="eq-work-Tds"/> is simply <m>d\mathbf{r}</m> by
      <xref ref="eq-unit-tangent"/>, and we arrive at the compact form
      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-work-dr" number="yes">
          W=\int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{T}\,ds
            =\int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}.
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      In practice, once the curve is parametrized by
      <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m> for <m>a\le t\le b</m>,
      <xref ref="eq-work-dr"/> becomes
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r} \amp
            =\int_a^b \mathbf{F}\bigl(\mathbf{r}(t)\bigr)\cdot
             \frac{d\mathbf{r}}{dt}\,dt
        </mrow>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-work-parametrized" number="yes">
          \amp =\int_a^b \mathbf{F}\bigl(\mathbf{r}(t)\bigr)
                 \cdot\mathbf{v}(t)\,dt :
        </mrow>
      </md>
      evaluate the field along the curve, dot it with the velocity, and
      integrate over time. Work was our motivation, but any vector field
      <m>\mathbf{F}</m> can be integrated over a curve <m>C</m> in this
      fashion. <xref ref="fig-work-video"/> shows the accumulation of
      <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{T}\,ds</m> along a trajectory.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-work-video">
      <caption>
        As the object moves through the field
        <m>\mathbf{F}=\left\langle x,\ y\right\rangle</m> along the trajectory,
        the work <m>W=\int_C\mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> accumulates
        moment by moment from the tangential component of the force.
      </caption>
      <video xml:id="video-work-line-integral" width="80%"
             source="videos/work_line_integral_1080p.mp4"/>
    </figure>

    <p>
      There is a third, frequently used way to write the same integral.
      Assume the vector field has components
      <m>\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\left\langle M(x,y,z),\ N(x,y,z),\
         P(x,y,z)\right\rangle</m>. Since
      <m>d\mathbf{r}=\left\langle dx,\ dy,\ dz\right\rangle</m>,
      expanding the dot product gives the <term>differential form</term> of
      the line integral:
      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-work-differential" number="yes">
          \int_C \mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}
            =\int_C M\,dx+N\,dy+P\,dz.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      All three notations<mdash/><xref ref="eq-work-Tds"/>,
      <m>\int_C\mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>, and
      <m>\int_C M\,dx+N\,dy+P\,dz</m><mdash/>mean exactly the same thing.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- EXAMPLE 1: work along the twisted cubic.                           -->
  <example xml:id="ex-twisted-cubic-work">
    <title>Work along a twisted cubic</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate the work done by the force
        <md>
          \mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\left\langle y-x^2,\ z-y^2,\ x-z^2\right\rangle
        </md>
        over the curve <m>C</m>:
        <md>
          \mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle t,\ t^2,\ t^3\right\rangle
        </md>
        from <m>(0,0,0)</m> to <m>(1,1,1)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We use <xref ref="eq-work-parametrized"/>. The curve, shown in
        <xref ref="fig-twisted-cubic"/>, runs from
        <m>\mathbf{r}(0)=\left\langle 0,0,0\right\rangle</m> to
        <m>\mathbf{r}(1)=\left\langle 1,1,1\right\rangle</m>, so the
        parameter interval is <m>0\le t\le 1</m>. We begin by writing the
        force in terms of the parameter <m>t</m>, substituting
        <m>x=t</m>, <m>y=t^2</m>, <m>z=t^3</m>:
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \mathbf{F} \amp = \left\langle y-x^2,\ z-y^2,\ x-z^2\right\rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \left\langle t^2-t^2,\ t^3-t^4,\ t-t^6\right\rangle
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \left\langle 0,\ t^3-t^4,\ t-t^6\right\rangle,
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        where the first component vanishes identically along
        this particular curve. The velocity vector is
        <md>
          \mathbf{v}(t)=\frac{d\mathbf{r}}{dt}
            =\left\langle 1,\ 2t,\ 3t^2\right\rangle.
        </md>
        Finally, the amount of work is
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          W \amp = \int_C\mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp =\int_0^1\left\langle 0,\ t^3-t^4,\ t-t^6\right\rangle
                 \cdot\left\langle 1,\ 2t,\ 3t^2\right\rangle\,dt
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \int_0^1\left(2t^4-2t^5+3t^3-3t^8\right)dt
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \frac{2}{5}-\frac{1}{3}+\frac{3}{4}-\frac{1}{3}
               = \frac{29}{60}.
        </mrow>
      </md>

      <figure xml:id="fig-twisted-cubic">
        <caption>
          The twisted cubic
          <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle t,\ t^2,\ t^3\right\rangle</m>,
          <m>0\le t\le 1</m>, from the origin to <m>(1,1,1)</m>. The dashed
          gray curve is its shadow on the plane <m>z=0</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-twisted-cubic" width="70%">
          <shortdescription>
            A curve in space rising from the origin to the point (1,1,1),
            with its shadow on the floor.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              A three-dimensional box with x, y, and z from 0 to 1.1. A
              thick blue curve starts at a red point at the origin, labelled
              (0,0,0), hugs the floor at first, then sweeps upward ever more
              steeply to a red point labelled (1,1,1) at the far upper
              corner. A dashed gray parabola on the floor shows the curve's
              shadow.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
            \begin{axis}[width=10cm, view={115}{20},
                xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                xmin=0, xmax=1.1, ymin=0, ymax=1.1, zmin=0, zmax=1.1,
                grid=major, grid style={gray!20}, clip=false]
              \addplot3[gray!65, dashed, line width=1.3pt, domain=0:1,
                        samples=60, samples y=0]
                  ({x}, {x^2}, {0});
              \addplot3[blue, very thick, domain=0:1, samples=80, samples y=0]
                  ({x}, {x^2}, {x^3});
              \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, red, mark size=1.6pt] coordinates
                  {(0,0,0) (1,1,1)};
              \node[anchor=south east, xshift=-3pt] at (axis cs:0,0,0) {\((0,0,0)\)};
              \node[above=3pt] at (axis cs:1,1,1) {\((1,1,1)\)};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- EXAMPLE 2: the gravitational field along a helix (from the         -->
  <!-- handwritten notes of 01.17/01.22).                                 -->
  <example xml:id="ex-helix-gravity">
    <title>Work done by the gravitational field along a helix</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate the line integral
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_C \amp \frac{-x}{\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{3/2}}\,dx
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp +\frac{-y}{\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{3/2}}\,dy
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp +\frac{-z}{\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{3/2}}\,dz
          </mrow>
        </md>
        over the curve
        <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle\cos t,\ \sin t,\ t\rangle</m> from
        <m>(1,0,0)</m> to <m>(1,0,2\pi)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        This integral is in the differential form
        <xref ref="eq-work-differential"/> with
        <m>\mathbf{F}=\langle M,N,P\rangle</m>, and we recognize the field:
        <me>\mathbf{F}
            =\frac{\langle -x,\,-y,\,-z\rangle}{\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{3/2}}
            =-\frac{\mathbf{r}}{\left\lVert\mathbf{r}\right\rVert^3},</me>
        the gravitational field <xref ref="eq-gravity-field"/>, drawn in
        <xref ref="fig-gravity-field"/>. The
        curve is a helix that climbs one full turn, from <m>(1,0,0)</m> at
        <m>t=0</m> to <m>(1,0,2\pi)</m> at <m>t=2\pi</m>; see
        <xref ref="fig-helix-gravity"/>.
      </p>

      <p>
        We write the field along the curve in terms of <m>t</m>. On the
        helix,
        <me>x^2+y^2+z^2=\cos^2 t+\sin^2 t+t^2=1+t^2,</me>
        so
        <me>\mathbf{F}\bigl(\mathbf{r}(t)\bigr)
            =\frac{1}{\left(1+t^2\right)^{3/2}}
             \langle -\cos t,\ -\sin t,\ -t\rangle.</me>
        The velocity vector is
        <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle -\sin t,\ \cos t,\ 1\rangle</m>, and the dot
        product collapses nicely:
        <me>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{v}
            =\frac{\cos t\sin t-\sin t\cos t-t}{\left(1+t^2\right)^{3/2}}
            =\frac{-t}{\left(1+t^2\right)^{3/2}}.</me>
      </p>

      <p>
        Therefore, using the substitution <m>u=1+t^2</m>,
        <m>\tfrac{du}{2}=t\,dt</m>,
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_C M\,dx+N\,dy+P\,dz \amp
            = \int_{t=0}^{t=2\pi}\frac{-t}{\left(1+t^2\right)^{3/2}}\,dt
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = -\frac{1}{2}\int_{u=1}^{u=1+4\pi^2} u^{-3/2}\,du
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \left(-\frac{1}{2}\right)(-2)\,
                 \frac{1}{\sqrt{u}}\Bigg|_{u=1}^{u=1+4\pi^2}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1+4\pi^2}}-1\approx -0.84.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        The work is negative, and the picture explains why: the
        gravitational field always points toward the origin, while the helix
        climbs steadily away from it, so the tangential component of the
        force opposes the motion at every instant. The accumulation of this
        negative work is animated in <xref ref="fig-helix-gravity-video"/>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-helix-gravity">
        <caption>
          The helix <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle\cos t,\sin t,t\rangle</m>,
          <m>0\le t\le 2\pi</m>, climbing through the gravitational field
          <m>\mathbf{F}=-\mathbf{r}/\left\lVert\mathbf{r}\right\rVert^3</m>
          (orange arrows, direction only). At the same three points the
          velocity <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is drawn in green: at every one of
          them the field leans away from the direction of travel, which is
          why the work comes out negative.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-helix-gravity" width="70%">
          <shortdescription>
            One full turn of a helix rising from (1,0,0) to (1,0,2 pi), with
            arrows along it pointing toward the origin.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              A three-dimensional box with x and y from minus 1.4 to 1.4 and
              z from 0 to 7. A thick blue helix makes one full
              counterclockwise turn of radius one, starting at a red point
              labelled (1,0,0), t equals 0, on the floor and ending at a red
              point labelled (1,0,2 pi), t equals 2 pi, directly above it. A
              dashed gray unit circle on the floor is the helix's shadow.
              Three orange arrows attached to points of the helix, labelled
              F, point inward and downward, toward the origin. At the same
              three points three green arrows, labelled v, point along the
              helix in the direction of travel, upward and around; each
              green arrow makes an obtuse angle with the orange one beside
              it.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
            \begin{axis}[width=10cm, view={115}{15},
                xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                xmin=-1.4, xmax=1.4, ymin=-1.4, ymax=1.4, zmin=0, zmax=7,
                grid=major, grid style={gray!20}, clip=false]
              % shadow: unit circle on the ground
              \addplot3[gray!50, dashed, thin, domain=0:360, samples=90, samples y=0]
                  ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {0});
              % the helix, one full turn
              \addplot3[blue, very thick, domain=0:360, samples=140, samples y=0]
                  ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {pi*x/90/2});
              % a few field vectors F = -r/|r|^3 along the curve
              % (direction only), and the velocity at the same points
              \addplot3[orange, line width=1.5pt, -{Stealth[length=5pt]},
                  quiver={u=\thisrow{u}, v=\thisrow{v}, w=\thisrow{w}}]
                  table[row sep=\\] {%
x y z u v w\\
0 1 1.5708 0 -0.43 -0.68\\
-1 0 3.1416 0.24 0 -0.76\\
0 -1 4.7124 0 0.17 -0.78\\
};
              \addplot3[green!55!black, line width=1.5pt,
                  -{Stealth[length=5pt]},
                  quiver={u=\thisrow{u}, v=\thisrow{v}, w=\thisrow{w}}]
                  table[row sep=\\] {%
x y z u v w\\
0 1 1.5708 -0.55 0 0.55\\
-1 0 3.1416 0 -0.55 0.55\\
0 -1 4.7124 0.55 0 0.55\\
};
              \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, red, mark size=1.6pt] coordinates
                  {(1,0,0) (1,0,6.2832)};
              \node[anchor=west, xshift=10pt, yshift=4pt] at (axis cs:1,0,0) {\((1,0,0),\ t=0\)};
              \node[anchor=south west, xshift=4pt] at (axis cs:1,0,6.2832) {\((1,0,2\pi),\ t=2\pi\)};
              \node[orange, anchor=east] at (axis cs:-1.4,-0.3,2.55)
                  {\(\mathbf F\)};
              \node[green!55!black, anchor=west]
                  at (axis cs:0.15,-1.05,5.5) {\(\mathbf v\)};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-helix-gravity-video">
        <caption>
          A particle climbs the helix through the gravitational field while
          the work <m>W(t)=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1+t^2}}-1</m> accumulates,
          reaching
          <m>\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1+4\pi^2}}-1\approx -0.84</m> at the top: the
          field opposes the motion the whole way.
        </caption>
        <video xml:id="video-helix-gravity" width="85%"
               source="videos/helix_gravity_work_1080p.mp4"/>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- Interactive exploration: the GeoGebra applet "VF-Fig1"             -->
  <!-- (geogebra.org/m/e2m6kghr), 599 x 488.                              -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-work-exploration">
    <title>Exploring the Work Integral</title>

    <p>
      The applet below carries every ingredient of
      <xref ref="eq-work-parametrized"/> at once: a curve <m>C</m>
      parametrized by <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m>, the velocity
      <m>\mathbf{v}(t)=d\mathbf{r}/dt</m> tangent to it, and a force field
      <m>\mathbf{F}</m> evaluated at the moving point. Work out what the
      integrand <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{v}</m> is doing before you
      compute anything.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-work-geogebra">
      <caption>
        A point climbs the curve <m>C</m> while the force field
        <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and the velocity <m>\mathbf{v}</m> are drawn at
        its current position. The work is the accumulated
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{v}</m>, so the angle between the two
        arrows is what decides its sign.
      </caption>
      <interactive geogebra="e2m6kghr"
                   width="100%" aspect="599:488"
                   material-width="599" material-height="488">
        <instructions>
          <p>
            Drag the slider to move the point along the curve <m>C</m>, and
            watch the two arrows attached to it: <m>\mathbf{F}</m>, the
            force the field exerts there, and <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, the
            velocity, which is tangent to <m>C</m> and points in the
            direction of travel. Rotate the picture by dragging the
            background if you want to see the configuration from another
            angle.
          </p>
          <p>
            At each position, ask yourself whether the angle between
            <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is acute, obtuse, or
            right<mdash/>that is, whether <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{v}</m>
            is positive, negative, or zero<mdash/>and whether the answer
            changes as the point climbs. That single observation settles the
            sign of <m>\int_C\mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> without any
            integration.
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The next question asks you to read off the sign of a work integral
      from a picture alone, with no computation. The field in
      <xref ref="fig-two-paths-field"/> is the radial field
      <xref ref="eq-radial-field"/> we started the section with, and the two
      paths are closed curves centred at the origin.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-two-paths-field">
      <caption>
        Two closed paths <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> in a force field. The
        arrows on the curves give their orientation, and the sliders change
        the shape of <m>C_1</m>.
      </caption>
      <interactive geogebra="arkzz25s"
                   width="100%" aspect="758:475"
                   material-width="758" material-height="475">
        <instructions>
          <p>
            Notice the orientation of each curve. Drag the points on a curve
            to change where it starts, and use the sliders to reshape
            <m>C_1</m>. At any point of either curve, compare the direction
            of the field arrow there with the direction in which the curve
            is being traversed.
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>

    <exercise xml:id="q-work-sign-two-paths">
      <title>Positive, negative, or zero?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In <xref ref="fig-two-paths-curves"/>, determine whether the work
          done by the force field along the two paths <m>C_1</m> and
          <m>C_2</m> is positive, negative, or zero.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-two-paths-curves">
          <caption>
            The two closed paths of
            <xref ref="fig-two-paths-field"/> in the radial field
            <xref ref="eq-radial-field"/>: the ellipse <m>C_1</m> and the
            circle <m>C_2</m>. Notice the orientation of each curve.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="image-two-paths-curves" width="65%">
            <shortdescription>
              An ellipse and a circle centred at the origin, drawn in a field
              of arrows that point away from the origin, each curve carrying
              an arrowhead that gives its direction of travel.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                The plane with x and y from minus 5 to 5, filled with blue
                arrows that point straight away from the origin and grow
                longer with the distance from it. A green ellipse crosses the
                x axis at plus and minus three and the y axis at plus and
                minus one; its arrowhead, just above the origin, points in the
                negative x direction, so the ellipse is traversed
                counterclockwise. A black circle of radius two is centred at
                the origin; its arrowhead, in the upper right, points down and
                to the right, so the circle is traversed clockwise. A red dot
                marks the origin, where the field is the zero vector.
              </p>
            </description>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="two-paths-curves">
              <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                <definition>F(x,y) = (0.22*x, 0.22*y)</definition>
                <definition>c1(t) = (3*cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
                <definition>c2(t) = (2*cos(t), -2*sin(t))</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-5.4,-5.4,5.4,5.4)" aspect-ratio="1">
                  <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>

                  <group at="field">
                    <repeat parameter="i=-4..4">
                      <repeat parameter="j=-4..4">
                        <vector v="F(i,j)" tail="(i,j)" stroke="blue"
                                thickness="2"/>
                      </repeat>
                    </repeat>
                  </group>

                  <group at="c2">
                    <parametric-curve function="c2" domain="(0,2*pi)"
                                      closed="yes" stroke="black"
                                      thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                      arrow-location="5.24"/>
                    <label anchor="(-1.45,-1.45)" alignment="sw"
                           clear-background="yes">
                      <m>C_2</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="c1">
                    <parametric-curve function="c1" domain="(0,2*pi)"
                                      closed="yes" stroke="#009E73"
                                      thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                      arrow-location="1.35"/>
                    <label anchor="(-3.15,0.5)" alignment="nw"
                           color="#009E73" clear-background="yes">
                      <m>C_1</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="origin">
                    <point p="(0,0)" size="4.5" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>

                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure"
                              text="Two closed paths drawn in the radial field
                                    F of x and y equals the vector x, y, whose
                                    arrows all point straight away from the
                                    origin and grow with the distance from it.
                                    Each path carries one arrowhead showing
                                    the direction in which it is traversed">
                    <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                    <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                    <annotation ref="field"
                                text="The grid of field vectors, each pointing
                                      radially outward with length
                                      proportional to the distance from the
                                      origin"/>
                    <annotation ref="c1"
                                text="The path C 1, an ellipse crossing the x
                                      axis at plus and minus three and the y
                                      axis at plus and minus one. Its
                                      arrowhead sits just above the origin and
                                      points in the negative x direction, so C
                                      1 is traversed counterclockwise"
                                sonify="yes"/>
                    <annotation ref="c2"
                                text="The path C 2, a circle of radius two
                                      centred at the origin. Its arrowhead
                                      sits in the upper right and points down
                                      and to the right, so C 2 is traversed
                                      clockwise"
                                sonify="yes"/>
                    <annotation ref="origin"
                                text="The origin, where the field is the zero
                                      vector"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Zero along both paths<mdash/>but for two different reasons.
        </p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The field is <m>\mathbf{F}=\left\langle x,\ y\right\rangle</m>,
          which at every point aims straight away from the origin.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>The circle <m>C_2</m>.</term> A circle centred at the origin
          keeps a constant distance from it, so the direction of travel is
          everywhere perpendicular to the outward direction:
          <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{T}=0</m> at <em>every</em> point of the
          curve. The integrand of <xref ref="eq-work-Tds"/> vanishes
          identically, so the work is zero. Reversing the orientation
          changes nothing<mdash/>zero either way.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>The ellipse <m>C_1</m>.</term> Here the integrand does
          <em>not</em> vanish: on the stretches where the curve moves away
          from the origin the field pushes it along and
          <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{T}\gt 0</m>, while on the stretches
          where it moves back inward
          <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\mathbf{T}\lt 0</m>. Over one full circuit the
          curve returns to where it started, so the outward stretches and
          the inward stretches cancel exactly and the total is again zero.
          Reversing the orientation swaps which stretches are positive, and
          the total is still zero.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second argument is the more powerful one: it never used the
          particular shape of <m>C_1</m>, only that the path is closed and
          that how much the field pushes depends solely on the distance from
          the origin. That is the property of a <em>conservative</em> field,
          which is the subject of the next section.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

</section>
